% Cut and if-then-else as control-flow tools -- the classic
% "commit to the first matching case" idiom.

% Using if-then-else: one clause, no choice points left behind at all.
classify(X, negative) :- X < 0, !.
classify(0, zero) :- !.
classify(_, positive).

% Same idea, written with nested if-then-else instead of cut+clause-order.
sign(X, R) :- (X < 0 -> R = negative ; X =:= 0 -> R = zero ; R = positive).

% max/3 with a cut: once we know which argument is bigger, there is
% only ever one right answer, so commit to it.
max(X, Y, X) :- X >= Y, !.
max(X, Y, Y) :- X < Y.

% once/1 vs real cut, side by side: cut commits to the enclosing
% clause (picked_cut(X) only ever gives X=a); once/1 only limits its
% OWN goal's solution count, it never touches clause selection at all
% (picked_once(X) still finds both X=a and X=b on backtracking).
picked_cut(a) :- !.
picked_cut(b).

picked_once(a) :- once(true).
picked_once(b).
